{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1536 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "13d644374bd1dcdc4a162c43304ffb8acabcde6ba7062a55f3a99737817a2c3a" :edb/db-before-t 1535 :edb/db-after-t 1536 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534848 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:02:45.877Z" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185701 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185702 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185703 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185704 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185705 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440854 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440856 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440860 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440872 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440876 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186185700 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440860 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188007 1071 "Suppose we want to measure the volume of a shoe box. What units could we use?\n\n1. $\\textrm{cm}^2$\n2. $\\textrm{cm}^3$\n3. $\\textrm{kg}$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188007 1071 "Suppose we want to measure the volume of a shoe box. What units could we use?\n\nI. $\\textrm{cm}^2$\n\nII. $\\textrm{cm}^3$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{kg}$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188007 1074 "We can measure length in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$. When measuring lengths in centimeters, volumes are measured in $\\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm}$, which is usually written as $\\textrm{cm}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188007 1074 "We can measure length in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}).$ When measuring lengths in centimeters, volumes are measured in $\\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm},$ which is usually written as $\\textrm{cm}^3.$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188008 1071 "![](images/7e/7e21a9afeb5a6c102a8426255b17112479144845be87f121435c8e27078610ab.png)\n\n What is the volume of the cube shown in the diagram above?" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188008 1071 "![](images/7e/7e21a9afeb5a6c102a8426255b17112479144845be87f121435c8e27078610ab.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube shown in the diagram above?" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188008 1074 "To find the volume $V$ of a cube, we cube its side length. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= (5m)^{3} \\\\\n&= 5m \\cdot 5m \\cdot 5m \\\\\n&= (5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (m \\cdot m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 125m^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188008 1074 "To find the volume $V$ of a cube, we cube its side length. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(5\\,\\text{m})^{3} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m}\\times 5\\,\\text{m}\\times 5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =(5\\times 5\\times 5)\\times (\\text{m}\\times \\text{m}\\times \\text{m}) \\\\  & =125\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188009 1071 "![](images/7e/7e300a90dbfda5a9210cec502c2879b7950e2765c433eb6ee6284e74db3cd220.png)\n\n What is the area of the square above?" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188009 1071 "![](images/7e/7e300a90dbfda5a9210cec502c2879b7950e2765c433eb6ee6284e74db3cd220.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the square above?" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188009 1074 "To calculate the area $A$ of a square, we square its side length. Therefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= (5ft)^{2} \\\\\n&= 5ft \\cdot 5ft \\\\\n&= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (ft \\cdot ft) \\\\\n&= 25ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186188009 1074 "To calculate the area $A$ of a square, we square its side length. Therefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(5\\,\\text{ft})^{2} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}\\times 5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\  & =(5\\times 5)\\times (\\text{ft}\\times \\text{ft}) \\\\  & =25\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440897 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440898 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440899 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440902 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440900 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440901 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440903 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440904 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188466 1050 420.0 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186188570 1074 "We can measure length in inches $(\\text{in}).$ When measuring lengths in inches, volumes are measured in $\\text{in}\\times \\text{in}\\times \\text{in},$ which is usually written as ${\\text{in}}^{3}.$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189141 1061 38472 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186189141 1061 633751 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189383 1064 "**Area** measures how much space there is on a flat surface. In this lesson, we'll learn about the various measurement units of area and how they relate to units of length.\n\nConsider a square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3feaac95102a8a3014b7ab114adb97d5a0267a5c654c584bff03152801c807c2.png)\n\nTo find the area of a square, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the area of this square is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nThe unit of area $(\\textrm m^2)$ we used is called a **square meter.** In this case, we say that the area of our square is **one square meter.**\n\nIn previous lessons, we learned that length can also be measured in kilometers $(\\textrm{km})$, centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$, and millimeters $(\\textrm{mm})$. Each of these length units has a corresponding area unit:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{km}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{km}^2$, or **one square kilometer**.\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, or **one square centimeter**.\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$, or **one square millimeter**.\n\nThe same idea applies when measuring area in customary units. For example:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^2$, or **one square inch**.\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$, or **one square foot**." 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186189383 1064 "**Area** measures how much space there is on a flat surface. In this lesson, we'll learn about the various measurement units of area and how they relate to units of length. \n\nConsider a square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/3f/3feaac95102a8a3014b7ab114adb97d5a0267a5c654c584bff03152801c807c2.png)\n\nTo find the area of a square, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the area of this square is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2.\n$$\n\nThe unit of area $(\\textrm m^2)$ we used is called a **square meter.** In this case, we say that the area of our square is **one square meter.**\n\nIn previous lessons, we learned that length can also be measured in kilometers $(\\textrm{km}),$ centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ and millimeters $(\\textrm{mm}).$ Each of these length units has a corresponding area unit:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{km}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{km}^2,$ or **one square kilometer**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ or **one square centimeter**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{mm}^2,$ or **one square millimeter**.\n\nThe same idea applies when measuring area in customary units. For example:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^2,$ or **one square inch**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2,$ or **one square foot**." 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189385 1064 "**Volume** measures how much three-dimensional space there is inside something.\n\nConsider a cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0006ee19c09fcf56ad032675f5f71ca8e49fc4c554a169e17bff0a09f2bbb6a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of a cube, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the volume of this cube is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^3\n$$\n\nThe unit of volume $(\\textrm m^3)$ we used is called a **cubic meter.** In this case, we say that the volume of our cube is **one cubic meter.**\n\nAs with area, each unit of length has a corresponding volume unit. For example:\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^3$, or **one cubic centimeter**.\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^3$, or **one cubic inch**." 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186189385 1064 "**Volume** measures how much three-dimensional space there is inside something. \n\nConsider a cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/f0/f0006ee19c09fcf56ad032675f5f71ca8e49fc4c554a169e17bff0a09f2bbb6a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of a cube, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the volume of this cube is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^3.\n$$\n\nThe unit of volume $(\\textrm m^3)$ we used is called a **cubic meter.** In this case, we say that the volume of our cube is **one cubic meter.**\n\nAs with area, each unit of length has a corresponding volume unit. For example:\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^3,$ or **one cubic centimeter**.\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^3,$ or **one cubic inch**." 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189389 1064 "A square is a special type of rectangle whose length and width are equal. For example, consider the square below.\n\n![](images/a9/a92233299ba67fec2a7712c88afcabeff3973dda07afdd7f91c085c4564e2aa1.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this square equals $3\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nSince a square is a special type of rectangle, we find its area by multiplying its length and width. However, since its length and width are equal, we can find its area by *squaring* its side length:\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(3\\,\\text{m})^{2}\\text{ } \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =(3\\times 3)\\times (\\text{m}\\times \\text{m}) \\\\  & =9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189389 1064 "To calculate the area of a **rectangle**, we multiply its length by its width.\n\nFor example, let's calculate the area of the rectangle shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f44bfcd63ff10aba31a20082c260c6a1af773e7165b8d8d55db70e5fd0a09f6.png)\n\nFor this rectangle, its length is $6\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and its height is $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$. Therefore, the area of the rectangle equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 6cm \\cdot 3cm \\\\\n&= (6 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (cm \\cdot cm) \\\\\n&= 18cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA square is a special type of rectangle whose length and width are equal. For example, consider the square below.\n\n![](images/63/63df4de7035288e9244fc111e546fa71cac312af5ec157075e1931b6835395b7.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this square equals $3\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n\nSince a square is a special type of rectangle, we find its area by multiplying its length and width. However, since its length and width are equal, we can find its area by *squaring* its side length:\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= (3m)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3m \\cdot 3m \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 9m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186189389 1065 6369 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186189389 1065 15346 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189433 1064 "A **cube** is a special type of rectangular solid whose length, width, and height are equal. \n\n![](images/e6/e696b4b9875052f18f394df4f0c6fb867b599a8484ac04acfca021ade9618a49.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this cube equals $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\nSince a cube is a special type of rectangular solid, we find its volume by multiplying its length, width, and height. However, since its length, width, and height are all equal, we can find its volume by *cubing* its side length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(2\\,\\text{cm})^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm}) \\\\  & =(2\\times 2\\times 2)\\times (\\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}) \\\\  & =8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186189433 1064 "To calculate the volume of a **rectangular solid**, we multiply its length, width, and height.\n\nFor example, let's compute the volume of the rectangular solid below.\n\n![](images/06/06088f818e6d95b156abaf47439125a2c64140c63f56d4e9730a6fd42473e72f.png)\n\nFor this rectangular solid, its length is $7\\,\\textrm m$, its width is $2\\,\\textrm m$, and its height is $3\\,\\textrm m$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of this solid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 7m \\cdot 2m \\cdot 3m \\\\\n&= (7 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (m \\cdot m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 42m^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **cube** is a special type of rectangular solid whose length, width, and height are equal.\n\n![](images/e6/e696b4b9875052f18f394df4f0c6fb867b599a8484ac04acfca021ade9618a49.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this cube equals $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nSince a cube is a special type of rectangular solid, we find its volume by multiplying its length, width, and height. However, since its length, width, and height are all equal, we can find its volume by *cubing* its side length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= (2cm)^{3} \\\\\n&= (2cm) \\cdot (2cm) \\cdot (2cm) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2) \\cdot (cm \\cdot cm \\cdot cm) \\\\\n&= 8cm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534848 17592186045417 false] [17592186440852 1080 "900\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440852 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440852 1083 #uuid "05a416c3-5a8b-5bb4-9e2e-d5d5e3890c12" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440853 1080 "900\\,\\text{ft}" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440853 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440853 1083 #uuid "06582eb3-c27f-5092-9e9b-b1e567c5c91d" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440854 1080 "{\\text{in}}^{2}" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440854 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440854 1083 #uuid "0862cd3e-b3e4-5597-aea9-b56f814586e1" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440855 1080 "\\text{kg}" 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440855 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534848 17592186045417 true] [17592186440855 1083 #uuid "092e3085-a7b2-5432-aa1b-416e63c08efe" 13194139534848 175921860